WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.730
Two giant empires from opposite ends of the world met face to face only once in history.

2
00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:13.060
When that clash, lasting a few days, was over, the losing side left behind not just an army but its own greatest secret.

3
00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:18.790
A handful of men walking among the prisoners would change the way humanity stores knowledge forever.

4
00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:38.800
Kanal yönergesi gereği bu bir çeviri kanalı: metin sıfırdan yazılmadı, aynı günün `tarih.txt` bölümünün İngilizce çevirisidir (kaynağa sadık; içerik eklenmedi/çıkarılmadı).

5
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:44.370
Kanal dili `en` olduğu ve Türkçe okunuş zinciri atlandığı için sayılar yazıyla verildi.

6
00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:54.420
If you could have looked down on the map of the world in the middle of the eighth century, you would have seen two enormous shadows, born at opposite ends of Asia, growing slowly toward one another.

7
00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:57.590
In the east stood the Tang dynasty.

8
00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:10.520
Its capital, Chang'an, was the most crowded city on the planet in that age; along its streets a Sogdian merchant, a Turkic soldier, a Japanese student, a Persian musician and an Indian monk walked side by side.

9
00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:19.130
The reign of Emperor Xuanzong had passed its fortieth year, and the dynasty had grown used to regarding itself as the center not only of China but of the known world.

10
00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:32.220
Tang's western frontier now lay far beyond the Great Wall: the garrisons established across the Tarim Basin — the lines of Kucha, Khotan, Kashgar and Karashahr — had been gathered under the name of the Anxi Protectorate,

11
00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:36.670
extending the empire's arm all the way to the foot of the Pamir mountains.

12
00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:40.160
In the west there was an altogether different energy.

13
00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:47.970
In the year seven hundred fifty, the Damascus-based Umayyad dynasty was brought down by a revolution and the Abbasids took its place.

14
00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:53.860
The engine of that revolution had run not in Arabia but precisely in the east, in Khurasan.

15
00:01:53.860 --> 00:02:00.870
The man at the head of the movement was an extraordinary organizer whose origins are still debated today: Abu Muslim.

16
00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:08.680
When his black banners set out from Merv and overturned the ruling house, the center of gravity of the new caliphate shifted eastward as well.

17
00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:18.250
For the Abbasids, Transoxiana — the lands beyond the river Oxus — was no distant march, but the back garden of the house in which the dynasty had been born.

18
00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:26.540
The country caught between these two worlds was the territory shared today by Uzbekistan, Tajikistan, Kyrgyzstan and Kazakhstan.

19
00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:30.030
In that age this was nobody's provincial backwater.

20
00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:41.680
Samarkand, Bukhara, Panjikent, Tashkent and Ferghana were wealthy city-states, ringed by walls, their interiors covered with frescoes, each with its own kings and its own coinage.

21
00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:46.770
Most of their people were Sogdians: the undisputed merchant nation of the Silk Road.

22
00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:53.460
Sogdian was a language of trade valid from Dunhuang on the Chinese frontier to the northern shores of the Black Sea.

23
00:02:53.460 --> 00:03:02.230
These cities carried silk, glass, paper, horses, slaves, musk and silver; but what they really carried was information.

24
00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:10.840
Which pass lay under snow, where which khan's army stood, in which city the price of which commodity had risen — they knew all of it.

25
00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:16.090
There was a third power in the region, and it is often forgotten: the Tibetan Empire.

26
00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:24.060
In the seventh and eighth centuries Tibet had spilled over to the north of the Himalayas and had repeatedly threatened Tang's western garrisons.

27
00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:30.270
The whole of Tang's military calculation in Central Asia was in fact built on encircling Tibet.

28
00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:36.960
And the most fearless executor of that calculation was the commander of the Anxi Protectorate, Gao Xianzhi.

29
00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:44.290
Gao Xianzhi was the son of a family that had entered Tang service after the fall of the kingdom of Goguryeo on the Korean peninsula.

30
00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:50.020
Like his father he became a soldier, rose quickly, and mastered mountain warfare.

31
00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:53.350
He made his name in seven hundred forty-seven.

32
00:03:53.350 --> 00:04:02.440
That year he crossed the Pamirs to bring into line the kingdom of Little Balur — the region of Gilgit today — which had come to an understanding with the Tibetans.

33
00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:11.370
With thousands of soldiers he marched through passes above four thousand meters, across glacier beds, in a column that advanced as if threaded on a string.

34
00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:18.060
The military logic of the campaign may be argued over, but its logistics were astonishing; centuries later,

35
00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:24.270
archaeologists who combed the region hand's breadth by hand's breadth would still recall that march with amazement.

36
00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:30.320
When Gao returned, his name was circulating at the Tang court as the commander who crossed the mountains.

37
00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:33.650
But that success brought a dangerous habit with it.

38
00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:40.980
Gao Xianzhi had begun to build his relations with the small western states no longer through negotiation, but through punishment.

39
00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:47.350
The reports that went to Chang'an counted victories, and the caravans that went to the treasury carried plunder.

40
00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:53.880
The court's distance was a reward: no one could supervise what was being done four thousand kilometers away.

41
00:04:53.880 --> 00:05:04.570
As the distance between a caliphate marching east and a dynasty reaching west closed a little more each year, the cities lying exactly in the middle of that distance were trying to decide which side to lean on.

42
00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:07.580
Where the spark would come from was not clear.

43
00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:10.430
The only clear thing was that it would come.

44
00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:15.200
The spark came in the year seven hundred fifty, in Tashkent.

45
00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:26.850
In that age the city's name appears in Chinese records as Shi, and in the local tongue as Chach; it was a wealthy oasis town on the fertile bank of the Syr Darya, where the caravan roads crossed.

46
00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:33.060
It had its own ruler, and it had declared its loyalty to Tang, but that loyalty existed only on paper.

47
00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:43.910
According to Gao Xianzhi's reports, the ruler of Tashkent was not fulfilling his obligations as a vassal, and in his dispute with neighboring Ferghana he refused to recognize Tang's arbitration.

48
00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:47.400
Gao came before the city with his army.

49
00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:52.650
The siege did not last long, because an agreement was reached before battle was joined.

50
00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:58.380
The ruler of Tashkent accepted surrender in exchange for a guarantee of his life and of his city.

51
00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:00.430
The gates were opened.

52
00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:02.320
Then the word was broken.

53
00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:10.130
The Chinese sources themselves — even sources not hostile to Gao Xianzhi — write with unmistakable discomfort at this point.

54
00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:13.460
The troops that entered the city began to plunder.

55
00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:19.190
The treasury was emptied, the arsenals taken, those in no condition to resist were killed.

56
00:06:19.190 --> 00:06:24.120
The ruler was put in chains and sent thousands of kilometers east, to Chang'an.

57
00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:31.130
And there, as a ruler who had surrendered and been protected by a promise, he was executed by order of the imperial court.

58
00:06:31.130 --> 00:06:34.620
That execution brought no military advantage.

59
00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:37.310
Politically it was a catastrophe.

60
00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:44.960
For until that day, every minor dynasty of Transoxiana had been debating the same question: can Tang's word be trusted?

61
00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:51.810
The promise given at the gate of Tashkent and not kept had answered that question in a way that left nothing further to debate.

62
00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:59.620
In the Sogdian cities the news spread at the speed of a caravan — which is to say, at a surprising speed.

63
00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:03.110
The son of the dead ruler managed to escape.

64
00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:07.240
And the direction in which he fled determined the whole story.

65
00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:11.850
He did not go east and take refuge with Tang; he went west.

66
00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:18.540
First he traveled among the neighboring principalities of the region and told them what had happened, then he reached Samarkand.

67
00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:29.230
Sitting in Samarkand at that time as governor for the Abbasid caliphate was Ziyad ibn Salih, an experienced commander answerable to Abu Muslim's broad sphere of authority in Khurasan.

68
00:07:29.230 --> 00:07:34.160
For Ziyad ibn Salih this invitation was the opportunity he had been waiting for.

69
00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:40.370
The Abbasids had only just come to power and needed to prove their legitimacy on the eastern frontier.

70
00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:49.300
And what had arrived was not merely a refugee's complaint; behind it stood the support of a whole series of local dynasties alarmed by Tang's conduct.

71
00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:55.830
From Samarkand an army began to be prepared, moving northeast toward the valley of the Talas.

72
00:07:55.830 --> 00:08:02.840
The backbone of this army was formed by the Khurasani troops: disciplined soldiers who had won a revolution and knew how to fight.

73
00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:06.970
To them were joined local cavalry gathered from the region.

74
00:08:06.970 --> 00:08:11.420
When Gao Xianzhi learned of the movement, he did not wait.

75
00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:17.630
That was his character: rather than meeting a threat on his own ground, he preferred to go and march upon it.

76
00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:20.640
He set out westward from his base at Kucha.

77
00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:30.050
The distance he would cover was more than seven hundred kilometers, and the road ran along the northern skirts of the Tian Shan, in midsummer, across waterless plains.

78
00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:34.500
The composition of his army is critical for understanding what followed.

79
00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:43.110
At the core were the professional Tang soldiers of the Anxi garrisons; their number was in all likelihood between ten thousand and twenty thousand.

80
00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:47.720
Alongside them marched allied troops from Ferghana.

81
00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:52.810
And the most numerous group were the Karluks, one of the Turkic peoples of the region.

82
00:08:52.810 --> 00:08:56.940
According to some records the Karluks made up two thirds of the army.

83
00:08:56.940 --> 00:09:06.830
In other words, Gao Xianzhi's army bore the name of a Tang army, but the majority of its soldiers were not Tang subjects — they were allies with calculated interests.

84
00:09:06.830 --> 00:09:14.960
The two armies met in the month of July in the year seven hundred fifty-one, on the bank of the Talas river, near a settlement called Atlakh.

85
00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:24.690
This is the area around the border of present-day Kazakhstan and Kyrgyzstan, in the vicinity of the city of Taraz; the exact site of the battlefield is still disputed.

86
00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:34.900
The figures for the total number of soldiers are complete chaos: Chinese records put the other side at two hundred thousand, Arab records count the Chinese army at one hundred thousand.

87
00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:41.430
The estimate modern historians find plausible is far more measured — around thirty thousand on each side.

88
00:09:41.430 --> 00:09:47.320
But this much was certain: neither army knew the other, and both were sure of winning.

89
00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:52.090
The plain on the bank of the Talas river was too narrow for a great pitched battle.

90
00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:55.900
Perhaps that is why the fighting did not end in a single day.

91
00:09:55.900 --> 00:09:58.430
It lasted five days.

92
00:09:58.430 --> 00:10:02.560
Five days is an unusual span for the battles of that age.

93
00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:07.170
It meant two armies probing each other without being able to force a decision.

94
00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:17.220
The days probably passed in mutual raids by mounted archers, in lines closing and drawing apart, in inconclusive charges against ranks of infantry holding their positions.

95
00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:26.470
Tang's advantage lay in discipline: tight blocks of infantry, long spears, and the most feared weapon of the Chinese army, the crossbow.

96
00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:32.680
The Abbasid side's advantage lay in heavy cavalry and in the battle experience of the Khurasani troops.

97
00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:35.210
For five days neither could break the other.

98
00:10:35.210 --> 00:10:40.460
At night the fires of the two camps burned facing each other on the two sides of the valley.

99
00:10:40.460 --> 00:10:43.310
On the fifth day everything changed.

100
00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:45.680
The Karluks changed sides.

101
00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:49.810
This is one of the most cold-blooded recalculations in history.

102
00:10:49.810 --> 00:10:58.100
The Karluk chiefs decided, most likely, on the basis of exactly what they had seen in the first four days of fighting: the Tang army was not winning.

103
00:10:58.100 --> 00:11:04.950
And what had happened at Tashkent was still in their memory; even if it did win, how far could Tang's word be trusted?

104
00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:14.040
They looked at their own pastures, their own trade routes, their own future, and judged it wiser to stand on the west-facing side of the valley of the Talas.

105
00:11:14.040 --> 00:11:17.530
The Karluk cavalry was inside the Tang lines.

106
00:11:17.530 --> 00:11:22.140
They did not need to cross the front or break through enemy formations.

107
00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:27.870
They simply turned their horses' heads and attacked the rear of the army they had been marching beside.

108
00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:32.320
At that moment Gao Xianzhi's army was caught between two fires.

109
00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:44.130
In front, Ziyad ibn Salih's troops increased the pressure; behind, a downpour of cavalry that had been taken for allies plunged into the supply lines, the horses, the lightly protected auxiliary units.

110
00:11:44.130 --> 00:11:48.740
Tight blocks of infantry are strong only so long as they can hold their order.

111
00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:54.310
The blow from behind broke the order, and once the order broke, the battle was over.

112
00:11:54.310 --> 00:11:57.000
The rest was not fighting but dissolution.

113
00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:05.610
The Chinese sources give the losses as between twenty thousand and thirty thousand, and put the number of Tang soldiers who escaped alive at under two thousand.

114
00:12:05.610 --> 00:12:12.940
Arab records write far larger figures: up to forty-five thousand dead, more than twenty thousand taken prisoner.

115
00:12:12.940 --> 00:12:19.470
None of these numbers is reliable; as with all medieval armies, the figures swell to match the size of the victory.

116
00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:26.320
What is reliable is the outcome: Tang's expeditionary army in the west was scattered in the course of one afternoon.

117
00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:31.250
The flight produced a scene recounted more often than the battle itself.

118
00:12:31.250 --> 00:12:34.740
The retreating crowd jammed in a narrow pass.

119
00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:45.590
Li Siye, one of Gao Xianzhi's commanders, began beating the fleeing allied Ferghanan soldiers with the staff in his hand, dragging horses and men off the road to clear a way for his commander.

120
00:12:45.590 --> 00:12:56.280
According to the records, another commander, Duan Xiushi, turned to him and said, roughly, that trampling over an ally to save your own life is not the work of a brave man.

121
00:12:56.280 --> 00:13:00.890
Li Siye stopped, and the two of them together brought the rest through the pass.

122
00:13:00.890 --> 00:13:07.580
The number of Tang soldiers who came out of that pass alive was a small fraction of those who had gone into battle that morning.

123
00:13:07.580 --> 00:13:10.270
Gao Xianzhi survived.

124
00:13:10.270 --> 00:13:13.600
He returned east, to the lands of Anxi.

125
00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:18.690
But he left behind not only an army; he left a policy behind as well.

126
00:13:18.690 --> 00:13:24.900
After the summer of seven hundred fifty-one, Tang armies never again crossed to the west of the Pamirs.

127
00:13:24.900 --> 00:13:30.310
The face Transoxiana turned toward China closed, and it did not open again.

128
00:13:30.310 --> 00:13:36.520
And on the battlefield, among the dead, a long line of human beings formed, bound in chains.

129
00:13:36.520 --> 00:13:39.530
The prisoners were being marched west.

130
00:13:39.530 --> 00:13:49.580
Among them were ordinary soldiers; and among the soldiers, as a consequence of the mobilization system of that age, were people who had joined the army with their civilian trades.

131
00:13:49.580 --> 00:13:50.990
Weavers.

132
00:13:50.990 --> 00:13:52.880
Painters.

133
00:13:52.880 --> 00:13:55.410
Goldsmiths.

134
00:13:55.410 --> 00:13:59.060
And a clerk who would carry the rest of the story.

135
00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:02.070
One of the prisoners was named Du Huan.

136
00:14:02.070 --> 00:14:06.360
That we know of him today is pure coincidence.

137
00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:12.730
After the battle Du Huan was taken to Abbasid territory, and he stayed there a full ten years.

138
00:14:12.730 --> 00:14:19.260
What he saw he recounted, after his return, in a book: the Jingxingji, the Record of Travels.

139
00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:21.150
The book was lost.

140
00:14:21.150 --> 00:14:31.840
But Du Huan's relative, the statesman Du You, took excerpts from that travel account while writing the Tongdian, the Comprehensive Institutions, a vast encyclopedia of government.

141
00:14:31.840 --> 00:14:38.050
What remains in our hands today is those excerpts: a residue of roughly fifteen hundred characters.

142
00:14:38.050 --> 00:14:44.900
The only surviving direct testimony written by a Chinese prisoner about the Abbasid world before Baghdad was founded.

143
00:14:44.900 --> 00:14:51.110
The city Du Huan describes was Kufa, one of the administrative centers of the caliphate at that time.

144
00:14:51.110 --> 00:14:54.920
His account is curious and surprisingly impartial.

145
00:14:54.920 --> 00:15:06.570
He notes the local faith, the order of worship, the month of fasting, the dress of women and of men, the wealth of the markets, the arrangement of rice and bread, even the provisions made for the sick and the poor.

146
00:15:06.570 --> 00:15:11.980
He does not belittle, he does not mock; he writes like an observer.

147
00:15:11.980 --> 00:15:16.750
But for our story the most valuable lines are the ones in which he counts people.

148
00:15:16.750 --> 00:15:24.400
Du Huan writes that in Kufa he met Chinese craftsmen who, like himself, had been brought from the Talas, and he names some of them.

149
00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:28.530
Two painters from the capital region, Fan Shu and Liu Ci.

150
00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:32.660
Weavers from the Hedong region, among them Lü Li.

151
00:15:32.660 --> 00:15:36.150
He also speaks of masters who worked gold and silver.

152
00:15:36.150 --> 00:15:45.240
So among the prisoners of the battle of Talas there really were craftsmen, they really were working in Abbasid cities, and some of them had built a new life there.

153
00:15:45.240 --> 00:15:49.210
Now let us come to the most striking aspect of this testimony.

154
00:15:49.210 --> 00:15:51.900
Du Huan counted the painters.

155
00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:54.110
He counted the weavers.

156
00:15:54.110 --> 00:15:57.120
He counted the goldsmiths.

157
00:15:57.120 --> 00:15:59.650
He never mentioned papermakers.

158
00:15:59.650 --> 00:16:10.980
In the text of the only eyewitness who was taken prisoner at the Talas, who lived there for ten years, who wrote down the trades of his countrymen one by one, there is not a single line about the making of paper.

159
00:16:10.980 --> 00:16:14.790
So where does that famous story come from?

160
00:16:14.790 --> 00:16:17.000
Its source is known.

161
00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:23.690
About three centuries after the battle, in the eleventh century, the man of letters al-Tha'alibi of Nishapur,

162
00:16:23.690 --> 00:16:32.620
in his book Lata'if al-Ma'arif — a collection of curious information — adds a note while explaining that the paper of Samarkand was of very high quality:

163
00:16:32.620 --> 00:16:37.870
this art was brought to the city by the Chinese prisoners taken by Ziyad ibn Salih.

164
00:16:37.870 --> 00:16:40.080
A single sentence.

165
00:16:40.080 --> 00:16:46.450
Written three hundred years later — pleasant, memorable, an inquisitive footnote.

166
00:16:46.450 --> 00:16:51.700
That single sentence passed into every history of paper written over the following thousand years.

167
00:16:51.700 --> 00:16:57.270
Modern research approaches this sentence with caution, and there is a solid reason for that.

168
00:16:57.270 --> 00:17:04.280
Paper cannot have come to Central Asia in seven hundred fifty-one, because before seven hundred fifty-one it was already there.

169
00:17:04.280 --> 00:17:10.810
The clearest proof of this is the archive found in the ruins of the fortress on Mount Mugh, in Tajikistan.

170
00:17:10.810 --> 00:17:20.220
The documents that the ruler of Panjikent, Devashtich, carried up the mountain during the Arab conquests were found there, and a portion of those documents was written on paper.

171
00:17:20.220 --> 00:17:24.670
Their dates go back roughly thirty years before the battle of Talas.

172
00:17:24.670 --> 00:17:32.320
In other words, the Sogdian rulers were using paper in their daily administrative correspondence before the battle ever took place.

173
00:17:32.320 --> 00:17:39.970
Other paper finds from this region, consisting of texts in Sogdian, Arabic and Chinese, point in the same direction.

174
00:17:39.970 --> 00:17:43.140
Paper itself is far older.

175
00:17:43.140 --> 00:17:51.430
According to the traditional account, its manufacture in China was presented to the emperor in the year one hundred five by the court official Cai Lun,

176
00:17:51.430 --> 00:17:56.840
while archaeological evidence has shown that simple kinds of paper were in use even before him.

177
00:17:56.840 --> 00:18:04.650
It is illogical in any case that a technology flowing along the Silk Road for six hundred years should have waited for the prisoners of a single battle.

178
00:18:04.650 --> 00:18:08.940
What is more, the technical details also strain the story.

179
00:18:08.940 --> 00:18:12.910
Chinese paper was made largely from tree bark fibers.

180
00:18:12.910 --> 00:18:22.480
The paper that developed in Transoxiana and in Iran, on the other hand, was produced largely from rags — that is, from worn-out linen and hemp cloth.

181
00:18:22.480 --> 00:18:27.410
This is not a copied method but a method rebuilt around local materials.

182
00:18:27.410 --> 00:18:37.940
That is what made Samarkand famous: its abundant running water, its water mills, the rags left over from its textile industry, and the skill of its masters in combining those three.

183
00:18:37.940 --> 00:18:42.550
For centuries, Samarkand paper was cited as the measure of quality.

184
00:18:42.550 --> 00:18:46.520
So the true story is not a secret stolen overnight.

185
00:18:46.520 --> 00:18:54.490
It is a slow transmission spread over decades, carried together by merchants, clerks, prisoners, migrants and craftsmen.

186
00:18:54.490 --> 00:18:59.740
The Talas was not the cause of that transmission; at most it was one of its accelerators.

187
00:18:59.740 --> 00:19:04.670
Neither the winner nor the loser of the battle enjoyed victory or defeat for long.

188
00:19:04.670 --> 00:19:12.960
Ziyad ibn Salih rebelled against his own superior, Abu Muslim, in the year after the Talas, in seven hundred fifty-two.

189
00:19:12.960 --> 00:19:16.610
His men abandoned him; he fled and was killed.

190
00:19:16.610 --> 00:19:25.220
Abu Muslim himself was put to death in seven hundred fifty-five, in the presence of Caliph al-Mansur, by order of the very state he had founded.

191
00:19:25.220 --> 00:19:29.190
In the east the disaster was far greater.

192
00:19:29.190 --> 00:19:36.840
In December of seven hundred fifty-five, An Lushan, Tang's most powerful frontier commander in the north, rose in revolt.

193
00:19:36.840 --> 00:19:47.850
The rebellion dragged the empire into a civil war lasting nearly ten years; the capital fell, the emperor fled from the capital, and the dynasty never returned to what it had been.

194
00:19:47.850 --> 00:19:55.340
The collapse visible in the population registers is so great that historians still argue today over how to read those figures.

195
00:19:55.340 --> 00:19:59.470
Gao Xianzhi was called back into service to put down this rebellion.

196
00:19:59.470 --> 00:20:06.640
He went east and, facing a rebel army vastly superior in numbers, chose to withdraw and hold the Tong Pass.

197
00:20:06.640 --> 00:20:10.770
Militarily it was the right decision; politically it was fatal.

198
00:20:10.770 --> 00:20:16.980
An accusation sent to the court presented the withdrawal as cowardice and the distribution of supplies as corruption.

199
00:20:16.980 --> 00:20:24.470
In January of seven hundred fifty-six, by imperial decree, he was executed before the eyes of his own soldiers.

200
00:20:24.470 --> 00:20:32.760
The commander who crossed the Pamirs had seen defeat at the Talas, but his own end came not at the hand of an enemy — it came from his own court.

201
00:20:32.760 --> 00:20:36.570
The rebellion emptied the Anxi garrisons as well.

202
00:20:36.570 --> 00:20:43.900
The troops in the west were summoned east, the Tang presence in the Tarim Basin first weakened and then passed into the hands of Tibet.

203
00:20:43.900 --> 00:20:50.270
China's return to Central Asia would wait a full thousand years, until the eighteenth century.

204
00:20:50.270 --> 00:20:56.160
As for the Talas itself, in the eyes of contemporaries it was not the great event we take it for today.

205
00:20:56.160 --> 00:21:03.970
In Arab historical writing the battle is passed over almost cursorily; no epic was written about it, no monument of victory was raised.

206
00:21:03.970 --> 00:21:08.100
It was recorded as one among dozens of clashes along the frontier.

207
00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:17.350
What carried it to the weight it has today is a line drawn in hindsight: after seven hundred fifty-one Transoxiana never again entered the Chinese sphere of influence,

208
00:21:17.350 --> 00:21:22.120
and the region entered upon a cultural orientation that would last for centuries.

209
00:21:22.120 --> 00:21:29.450
As for paper, its journey continued independently of the battle, and it made its real leap forty years after the Talas.

210
00:21:29.450 --> 00:21:38.860
In the years seven hundred ninety-four and seven hundred ninety-five, in Baghdad, in the reign of Caliph Harun al-Rashid, a paper manufactory was established.

211
00:21:38.860 --> 00:21:44.270
To understand why this mattered so much, one has to look at paper's competitors.

212
00:21:44.270 --> 00:21:49.360
Parchment was expensive; a single book required a great many animals.

213
00:21:49.360 --> 00:21:55.090
Papyrus was fragile, and was produced in practically one region only, in Egypt.

214
00:21:55.090 --> 00:22:05.940
Paper, by contrast, was cheap, could be made anywhere from local materials, and had one property that was vital for administration: because the ink on it worked its way in among the fibers,

215
00:22:05.940 --> 00:22:09.750
it could not be scraped off and altered as it could on parchment.

216
00:22:09.750 --> 00:22:13.560
For a state, this meant a record closed to forgery.

217
00:22:13.560 --> 00:22:18.330
Tax registers, court proceedings and title deeds moved onto paper.

218
00:22:18.330 --> 00:22:23.260
Then paper did something that could not have been expected of it: it produced a culture.

219
00:22:23.260 --> 00:22:29.950
A paper-sellers' market was established in Baghdad, and a profession was born of copying, selling and binding books.

220
00:22:29.950 --> 00:22:36.640
Translations made from Greek, from Syriac, from Persian and from Sanskrit multiplied on this cheap surface.

221
00:22:36.640 --> 00:22:43.810
Texts of mathematics, medicine, astronomy and philosophy turned into books whose copying no longer cost a fortune.

222
00:22:43.810 --> 00:22:50.820
By the tenth century, Baghdad's book market was on a scale that had no equal anywhere in the world.

223
00:22:50.820 --> 00:22:53.350
From there the road turned west.

224
00:22:53.350 --> 00:22:57.800
Workshops were founded in Damascus, Cairo and Fez.

225
00:22:57.800 --> 00:23:05.290
In the twelfth century, in Andalusia, the paper of Xàtiva came to be sought after in every port of the Mediterranean.

226
00:23:05.290 --> 00:23:13.260
At the end of the thirteenth century the mills in the Italian town of Fabriano began to work, and European paper entered mass production.

227
00:23:13.260 --> 00:23:23.950
What was needed for printing to become possible in Europe was not movable type alone; it was a cheap surface to print on, and that surface had fallen into place two centuries before Gutenberg.

228
00:23:23.950 --> 00:23:27.760
We now know that this whole chain did not begin on a battlefield.

229
00:23:27.760 --> 00:23:34.450
But there is a reason the legend has lived so long: the human mind wants great changes to have a great moment.

230
00:23:34.450 --> 00:23:42.580
A valley, five days, a cavalry that changes sides, and masters in chains — as a story it is flawless.

231
00:23:42.580 --> 00:23:45.750
The truth is slower and more modest.

232
00:23:45.750 --> 00:23:57.240
Technology is carried not in chests of plunder but in people's hands and habits; it takes generations, it is reinvented according to local materials, and who brought it is most often forgotten.

233
00:23:57.240 --> 00:24:05.050
At the Talas two empires drew a border, and that border still affects the cultural map of Central Asia today.

234
00:24:05.050 --> 00:24:09.340
But what really spilled out beyond the valley was not the border.

235
00:24:09.340 --> 00:24:18.110
Within the same century, in the workshops of the same geography, a substance spread that made everything written on it cheaper, more numerous and more lasting.

236
00:24:18.110 --> 00:24:22.720
Ask non-specialists who won the battle and most of them will not know.

237
00:24:22.720 --> 00:24:28.290
But what was written on that substance, thirteen hundred years later, we are still reading.

238
00:24:28.290 --> 00:24:32.100
Nöron History'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

239
00:24:32.100 --> 00:24:35.750
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
